WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.180
- Have you ever stressed
out about sending an email?

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.290
Rereading it to see if it sounds okay

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:09.060
or wondering if you added
too many exclamation points?

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.700
Or maybe you feel confident
when expressing yourself

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.170
via text message,

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.660
but you just can't seem to find the words

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.520
when it's time to make a phone call.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.370
But it isn't just about who
you're communicating with

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.890
or whether there's a
screen separating you.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:24.480
The TIME that passes

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.969
between each chunk of
language makes a huge impact.

00:00:27.969 --> 00:00:30.069
(pensive music)

00:00:30.069 --> 00:00:32.819
(clock ticking)

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:38.320
See, I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky
and this is "Other Words."

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.903
(upbeat music)

00:00:45.448 --> 00:00:47.781
- [Announcer] "Other Words."

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.540
- Think about speaking
or signing face-to-face

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.070
or talking on the phone.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:53.903
These are forms

00:00:53.903 --> 00:00:55.860
of synchronous communication,

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.350
conversations where all the
speakers are participating

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.120
at the same time.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.580
Asynchronous communication,
on the other hand

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.684
involves time passing
between conversational turns.

00:01:05.684 --> 00:01:08.370
That could be a few minutes
when you shoot a quick reply

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:09.480
back to an email

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.660
or even weeks between sending
and receiving a letter.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.240
Synchronous communication
tends to use signed

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.620
or spoken language

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.020
while most asynchronous
conversation happens

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.430
in written form.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.950
It would be pretty weird
if I said "Hi to you"

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.686
and then waited around a few
hours for you to respond.

00:01:25.686 --> 00:01:28.320
This difference allows
synchronous communication

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.150
to carry layers of meaning

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:33.270
that asynchronous conversation
can't do as easily.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:34.950
Intonation or altering the pitch

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.010
of our voices can convey
emotion, add emphasis

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.180
and even indicate

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.150
whether something's a
question or a declaration.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:45.030
Sign Language users can express
these messages in the speed

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:48.240
and size of their signs,
but intonation carries a lot

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.480
of subtle context about
what an utterance means.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:54.248
It's the difference between
"Come see me in my office!"

00:01:54.248 --> 00:01:57.450
and "Come see me in my office."

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.960
Though incomplete, there are
ways we try to replicate tone

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.540
in asynchronous written communication.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.120
Punctuation helps us
denote that we're asking

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.820
a question, where we might
otherwise use up speak.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.430
We can italicize words for emphasis,

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.868
type in all caps to indicate shouting

00:02:13.868 --> 00:02:18.450
or even use mixed case to connote
a sing-songy mocking tone.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.910
But written language
doesn't perfectly replicate

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.190
all the different nuances when we speak.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:25.620
Think about how hard it is to show sarcasm

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:29.130
through writing, and when we
don't all share the same ideas

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.610
about how to visually
represent tone, well,

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.299
that's why some people
still send those ominous

00:02:34.299 --> 00:02:37.230
text messages with a period at the end.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:40.770
Synchronous communication also
gives us immediate feedback

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:44.190
when our conversation
partners use "backchanneling".

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:47.023
Nodding, saying "Mm hm,"
"Okay," or "Really,"

00:02:47.023 --> 00:02:49.181
while other people are speaking

00:02:49.181 --> 00:02:52.110
gives them real-time confirmation
that they're being heard

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.700
and understood.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:55.710
And speaking of nods,

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.980
synchronous conversation
is full of gesture.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.040
The ways we move our
bodies, especially our hands

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.350
when speaking can add emotional context

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:05.700
to the words we're saying

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.090
and even convey ideas not
easily expressed through speech,

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:11.520
like when you're trying
to come up with a word

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.995
but you can't quite... y'know.

00:03:15.995 --> 00:03:17.670
Some gestures which linguists

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.710
call emblems carry meaning

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:23.220
without us having to speak at
all, like a thumbs up for yes

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.410
or shaking your head for no.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:28.200
Gestures help a listener
more fully understand

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.030
what a speaker is trying to say.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.240
But research shows that
gesture also helps the speaker

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.250
with cognition and fluency.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:36.270
Blind speakers,

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.910
even those who have never
seen gestures produced

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.980
will regularly gesture when speaking.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.590
But even without these linguistic tools,

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:47.220
asynchronous communication
has its own advantages.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:48.120
A time gap

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.670
between conversational
turns can give us space

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.670
to look up extra information,
double check our spelling

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.500
and read over our message a time

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:58.500
or five to really perfect
what we want to say.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.050
Asynchronous communication
can also do away

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:05.310
with filler words, those ums
and likes, and you knows,

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.560
we use when we're trying
to complete a thought.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:10.200
Fillers serve a useful linguistic purpose

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.450
when we're thinking,
but not done speaking.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.040
They let others know that it's still

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.740
our conversational turn, but since no one

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:18.420
is likely to interrupt
you when you're writing

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.910
a letter, there's no
need to fill the pauses

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:22.710
while you find the words
you're looking for.

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:24.810
With all these differences in synchronous

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:27.660
and asynchronous
communication, it's no wonder

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.289
that sending an email to your
coworker feel so fraught.

00:04:30.289 --> 00:04:34.230
Especially in this always
online, work from home era,

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:36.390
written communication doesn't feel quite

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.010
so asynchronous anymore.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:40.662
Text messages may
technically be asynchronous

00:04:40.662 --> 00:04:42.990
but if you've ever been left on read,

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:44.880
you know that we send texts with the hope

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:46.110
of getting quick replies

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.750
and even having exchanges in real-time.

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:50.430
Same thing with instant messaging apps

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.710
like Slack, Teams or Discord.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.630
If your boss Slacks you a question instead

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:56.100
of sending it in an email,

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:59.100
it probably feels like they
want an immediate response.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.460
Conversely, supposedly
synchronous forms like cell phone

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:05.220
and Zoom calls tend to have a slight delay

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.370
that can throw a wrench into
the normal conversational flow.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:09.630
People are more likely to talk

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:11.550
over each other and backchanneling

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:13.980
and filler words are less precise to use

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.650
which means that the ways that, sorry.

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:17.483
No, no, you go.

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:18.540
Oh, okay.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:19.710
I was just gonna say

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.560
that the ways we communicate
start to blend our toolkits

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.230
for asynchronous and
synchronous communication.

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:28.170
Take the length and formality
of conversational turns.

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.300
Some people message in long paragraphs

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.520
with full punctuation while others send

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.950
off several quick bursts
of texts in a row.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:37.710
Text reactions like pinning
an exclamation point

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:40.890
or a haha to another person's
message can also function

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:43.500
as a form of backchanneling.
According to linguists

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.830
Lauren Gawne and Gretchen
McCullough, we're even using

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:49.512
emojis as a form of gesture,
both literally like the

00:05:49.512 --> 00:05:52.800
thumbs up, clapping hands
or fingers crossed emoji.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.440
And in more figurative
and innovative ways.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.180
We don't have an IRL equivalent

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:00.870
of the upside down smiley face,
or a string of flame emojis

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:04.140
but they have broadly understood
meanings and text form.

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:05.137
As McCullough writes,

00:06:05.137 --> 00:06:08.220
"These emojis don't stand
in for individual words.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:11.280
Instead, they make writing
feel a lot more like talking."

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.680
We can take some of these
semi-synchronous tools

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.140
into other contexts
where they can be useful.

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:18.810
Think voice notes, which
add intonation back

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:20.910
to asynchronous text conversations

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:23.820
or a zoom call where instead
of taking yourself off mute

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:27.060
just to say mmm, you can
now react with an emoji.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:29.896
As the tools and timelines
we use to communicate change,

00:06:29.896 --> 00:06:33.662
the ways we use language are
evolving right along with them.

00:06:33.662 --> 00:06:35.880
And while we've all felt the friction

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:38.310
of switching between
asynchronous, asynchronous

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:40.020
and semi-synchronous tools,

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:42.750
we're developing our
linguistic toolkits to be able

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:45.646
to communicate more clearly all the time.

00:06:45.646 --> 00:06:48.229
(upbeat music)

00:06:58.620 --> 00:06:59.453
See?

